  "Question": "Let \\(n\\) be a positive integer such that \\(\\dfrac12+\\dfrac13+\\dfrac17+\\dfrac1n\\) is an integer. Which of the following statements is not true:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(2\\) divides \\(n\\)",
    "B": "\\(3\\) divides \\(n\\)",
    "C": "\\(6\\) divides \\(n\\)",
    "D": "\\(7\\) divides \\(n\\)",
    "E": "\\(n\\gt84\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "First combine the fixed fractions:\n\\[\n\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac17\n=\\frac{21+14+6}{42}\n=\\frac{41}{42}.\n\\]\nLet \\(S\\) be the full sum. Since \\(n\\) is a positive integer, \\(n\\ge1\\), so \\(0<1/n\\le1\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\frac{41}{42}<S\\le\\frac{83}{42}<2.\n\\]\nThe only integer in this range is \\(1\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\frac1n=1-\\frac{41}{42}=\\frac1{42},\n\\]\nso \\(n=42\\). This positive value does make the original sum equal to \\(1\\).\n\nThe number \\(42\\) is divisible by \\(2\\), \\(3\\), \\(6\\), and \\(7\\), but it is not greater than \\(84\\). Thus the statement that is not true is \\(\\boxed{n>84}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
